oikade check: Find what's wrong with your home, as a deploy would.

It runs the compiler, then the source checks, then the checks your home's binary
makes of itself, which are the ones a deploy makes, with your values from the
.env beside your go.mod and the environment, which wins. A value your code uses
that neither has is only a warning, so a pull request passes without them.

[1mUsage:[0m
  oikade check [--module DIR] [--env-file FILE]

[1mFlags:[0m
  --module DIR     Your home's module. (default .)
  --env-file FILE  Your values, from this file rather than the .env beside your
                   go.mod.
  --context NAME   The host to reach over its network API, from oikade login.
  --data-dir DIR   The host's data directory, on this machine. (default
                   /var/lib/oikade)

[1mExample:[0m
  oikade check
---
oikade dev: Run your home against simulated devices.

Your code gets your values, from the .env beside your go.mod and the
environment, which wins, as it gets them on the host.

[1mUsage:[0m
  oikade dev [--module DIR] [--env-file FILE]

[1mFlags:[0m
  --module DIR     Your home's module. (default .)
  --env-file FILE  Your values, from this file rather than the .env beside your
                   go.mod.

[1mExample:[0m
  oikade dev
  oikade dev --env-file test-host.env
---
